// golf/courses/oasissands.js - Oasis Sands, nine holes, in the format documented in
// golf/CLAUDE.md ("The hole-data format"). Yards throughout; x across the hole, y up it.
//
// GENERATED, not authored. These holes are TRACED from a recording of a real course: every
// coastline, waste edge and green here is the shape that course actually has, recovered per square
// metre and reduced to polygons.
//
// What is NOT a straight trace is here because an audit against Pine Valley and Red Mesa said so -
// they are hand-authored and known good, and where this course was wildly unlike both, it was
// wrong. Every such number below is theirs, not one picked to look right:
//
//   - The MOWN CORRIDOR is carved 12 yds either side of the playing line, with light rough to 24.
//     Traced, 25.6% of this course's corridor was bunker and 43.2% heavy rough against 2.5% and
//     ~22% on both references. The source's mown lines are not this route. Water and green are
//     never carved.
//   - GREENSIDE bunkers are the ones within 35 yds of the green, which is where Pine Valley's sit
//     (median 22). Every bunker here used to be a fairwayBunker, so the ones hugging a green played
//     at 88% power instead of 75%.
//   - The TREES SURFACE exists. The format is explicit that trees are two things - objects that
//     block a ball, and a surface giving the woods lie - and this course had only the objects.
//   - PALMS keep 16 yds off the line. PV puts 5 of 5298 trees inside that, RM 11 of 2014.
//   - GREEN SLOPES are authored, and say so: a recording carries no elevation. Each is a smooth
//     field - a fall to the front, how greens drain, plus two undulations - sampled as its downhill
//     gradient and seeded per hole. They were a single repeated value, which draws a green that
//     breaks the same way everywhere; the docs call that worse than no read at all.
//
// FIVE THINGS THE FIRST PLAYTEST OF THIS COURSE FOUND (2026-09-07, ~200 headless rounds against
// the real engine, cross-checked against Pine Valley and Red Mesa played the same way). Each is a
// difference from those two courses that nothing here checked for:
//
//   - EVERY HOLE HAD NO `green` SURFACE. `green.poly` was present, so every check that reads it
//     passed - but `surfaces` had only the collar, and the lie lookup and the renderer both walk
//     `surfaces`. So the putting surface was PAINTED IN THE COLLAR'S COLOUR and every putt on the
//     course was a FRINGE lie: 0.80 of the accuracy band and 1.55x the drag. `validateHole` now
//     refuses a hole without one (holes.js), with a probe in test.js section 2.
//   - THE GREEN SLOPE VECTORS POINTED UPHILL, on all nine. Format: each cell points DOWNHILL. All
//     27 greens on the other two courses fall toward the tee; all nine here fell away from it, in
//     both halves. The fields are near curl-free (|curl| 0.03-0.16 of their own gradient), i.e.
//     the gradient of a real height field with the sign inverted, so both components are negated.
//     Measured after: putting conversion at 3/6/10/20/30/45 ft now matches both other courses.
//   - 53 OF 264 TREES STOOD WHERE NO TREE MAY GROW: 39 in water, 12 in bunkers, 2 on the fairway.
//     `holes.js`'s NO_BELT_TREE filter never ran on them, because it only filters BELT trees and
//     this course emits every palm into the hand-placed `trees` array. One of the two on fairway
//     was a palm 10.1 yds from hole 7's pin, dead on the tee-to-pin line of a 159 yd par 3; no
//     tree on either other course is within 22 yds of any pin. Over 1,080 holes on the same
//     seeds, hole 7 went from 6.98 strokes to 5.39 - the largest single move on the course.
//   - THE `route` STOPPED 20-59 YDS SHORT OF EVERY PIN (Pine Valley and Red Mesa: 0-6). That is
//     what left the whole approach outside the palm-clearance rule above, and it aims the test
//     player at a waypoint rather than at the green. Every route now runs to the pin.
//   - `cardYards` WAS THE STRAIGHT TEE-TO-PIN LINE, to within 0.6 yds on all nine, where the
//     format says it is the PLAYING CENTRELINE and holegen.js measures exactly that. The four
//     doglegs read up to 59 yds short on the card. The par 4s and the par 5 now carry their route
//     length; the three par 3s keep the straight line, because a par 3 is played over its hazard
//     and its route here is the walking way round one.
//
//   - HOLE 3 CUT ITS HOLE 0.8 YDS FROM THE EDGE OF ITS OWN GREEN. Measured against the other 36
//     holes, whose tightest pin is 3.6 (Pine Valley 12) and whose medians are 8.7 and 12.5; this
//     course own next-tightest is 3.0. It is not only a pin nobody would cut - the green own
//     outline is stroked 1.2 yds wide, so the EDGE PAINTED OVER THE CUP, measured on the live
//     site as #91c451 (greenEdge) where every other pin reads #a6d861 (green). Moved 2.2 yds to
//     the nearest point with 3.0 yds of clearance, which is the minimum the references use; the
//     route last point and cardYards follow it. Par is untouched, so every stored oasissands:3
//     and oasissands3 record still means exactly what it meant.
//
// STILL OPEN, and deliberately not invented here: this course has NO greenside bunkers at all
// (Pine Valley 51, Red Mesa 55) - the nearest bunker to any pin is 74 yds. Adding some would mean
// drawing sand this trace does not have.

